{"annotationScore": 4.0, "comments": [{"commentType": "FUNCTION", "texts": [{"value": "Tubulin is the major constituent of microtubules, a cylinder consisting of laterally associated linear protofilaments composed of alpha- and beta-tubulin heterodimers. Microtubules grow by the addition of GTP-tubulin dimers to the microtubule end, where a stabilizing cap forms. Below the cap, tubulin dimers are in GDP-bound state, owing to GTPase activity of alpha-tubulin"}]}, {"cofactors": [{"cofactorCrossReference": {"database": "ChEBI", "id": "CHEBI:18420"}, "evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P68363", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "name": "Mg(2+)"}], "commentType": "COFACTOR"}, {"commentType": "SUBUNIT", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P07437", "source": "UniProtKB"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P69893", "source": "UniProtKB"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P99024", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Heterodimer of alpha and beta chains. A typical microtubule is a hollow water-filled tube with an outer diameter of 25 nm and an inner diameter of 15 nM. Alpha-beta heterodimers associate head-to-tail to form protofilaments running lengthwise along the microtubule wall with the beta-tubulin subunit facing the microtubule plus end conferring a structural polarity. Microtubules usually have 13 protofilaments but different protofilament numbers can be found in some organisms and specialized cells. Interacts with CIMAP3. Interacts with DIAPH1 (By similarity). Interacts with MX1 (By similarity). May interact with RNABP10 (By similarity). Interacts with CFAP157 (By similarity). Nascent tubulin polypeptide interacts (via beta-tubulin MREI motif) with TTC5/STRAP; this interaction results in tubulin mRNA-targeted degradation (By similarity)"}]}, {"commentType": "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION", "subcellularLocations": [{"location": {"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P07437", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "id": "SL-0090", "value": "Cytoplasm, cytoskeleton"}}]}, {"commentType": "DOMAIN", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P07437", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "The MREI motif is common among all beta-tubulin isoforms and may be critical for tubulin autoregulation"}]}, {"commentType": "PTM", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P99024", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Some glutamate residues at the C-terminus are polyglycylated, resulting in polyglycine chains on the gamma-carboxyl group. Glycylation is mainly limited to tubulin incorporated into axonemes (cilia and flagella) whereas glutamylation is prevalent in neuronal cells, centrioles, axonemes, and the mitotic spindle. Both modifications can coexist on the same protein on adjacent residues, and lowering polyglycylation levels increases polyglutamylation, and reciprocally. Cilia and flagella glycylation is required for their stability and maintenance. Flagella glycylation controls sperm motility"}]}, {"commentType": "PTM", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P99024", "source": "UniProtKB"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "Q71U36", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Some glutamate residues at the C-terminus are polyglutamylated, resulting in polyglutamate chains on the gamma-carboxyl group (By similarity). Polyglutamylation plays a key role in microtubule severing by spastin (SPAST). SPAST preferentially recognizes and acts on microtubules decorated with short polyglutamate tails: severing activity by SPAST increases as the number of glutamates per tubulin rises from one to eight, but decreases beyond this glutamylation threshold (By similarity). Glutamylation is also involved in cilia motility (By similarity)"}]}, {"commentType": "PTM", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P07437", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Phosphorylated on Ser-172 by CDK1 during the cell cycle, from metaphase to telophase, but not in interphase. This phosphorylation inhibits tubulin incorporation into microtubules"}]}, {"commentType": "SIMILARITY", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"}], "value": "Belongs to the tubulin family"}]}], "entryAudit": {"entryVersion": 149, "firstPublicDate": "2005-02-01", "lastAnnotationUpdateDate": "2026-11-04", "lastSequenceUpdateDate": "2004-07-05", "sequenceVersion": 1}, "entryType": "UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)", "extraAttributes": {"countByCommentType": {"COFACTOR": 1, "DOMAIN": 1, "FUNCTION": 1, "PTM": 3, "SIMILARITY": 1, "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION": 1, "SUBUNIT": 1}, "countByFeatureType": {"Binding site": 9, "Chain": 1, "Compositional bias": 1, "Cross-link": 2, "Modified residue": 17, "Motif": 1, "Region": 1}, "uniParcId": "UPI0000001229"}, "features": [{"description": "Tubulin beta chain", "featureId": "PRO_0000048245", "location": {"end": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 444}, "start": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 1}}, "type": "Chain"}, {"description": "Disordered", "evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000256", "id": "MobiDB-lite", "source": "SAM"}], "location": {"end": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 444}, "start": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 423}}, "type": "Region"}, {"description": "MREI motif", "evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P07437", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "location": {"end": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 4}, "start": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 1}}, "type": "Motif"}, {"description": "Acidic residues", "evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000256", "id": "MobiDB-lite", "source": "SAM"}], "location": {"end": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 444}, "start": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 429}}, "type": "Compositional bias"}, {"description": "", "evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "Q13509", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "featureCrossReferences": [{"database": "ChEBI", "id": "CHEBI:37565"}], "ligand": {"id": "ChEBI:CHEBI:37565", "name": "GTP"}, "location": {"end": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 11}, 